  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nAbove normal temperatures continue through tomorrow. A frontal \nboundary is slowly sagging south through the northern portions of \nthe CWA this afternoon. The front will continue to drift south this \nevening and should end up south of the CWA by the overnight hours. \nIt will then lift back north through the day tomorrow. The remainder \nof today looks drier than originally forecast with dry air \ninfiltrating the mid levels. Some redevelopment may occur later\nthis afternoon and evening but uncertainty is higher due to the\nmodels not picking up on the current situation very well. Some \nshowers and storms could form along and north of the front \ntomorrow. A small portion of the areas including our northern \nmost counties could see a few strong to severe storms with the \nmain threat being damaging winds. \n\nTomorrow night a cold front comes through and brings cooler and \nbreezier conditions for Friday. Due to cloud cover and a stronger \ncold air advection signal, have lowered highs slightly for Friday. \nFriday's highs look to be in the upper 50s and lower 60s with breezy \nnorthwest winds with gusts up to 30 mph (mainly for northwest \nportions of the area with lesser gusts elsewhere). Saturday highs \nlook to be in the 60s but Saturday night lows could be in the 30s \nand could bring a first frost for the season. Sunday then kicks off \na warming trend with highs coming back up to around normal or even \nslightly above by midweek.",
